ainer(sj) coordinatoragent: [ { \"objects\": [\"sj\"], \"interaction\": \"open sj\" }, { \"objects\": [\"f\"], \"interaction\": \"open f\" }, { \"objects\": [\"mc\"], \"interaction\": \"open mc\" }, { \"objects\": [\"cup_b\"], \"interaction\": \"grasp cup_b with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"cup_b\", \"counter\"], \"interaction\": \"lift cup_b from counter with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"cup_b\", \"s\", \"ds\"], \"interaction\": \"wash cup_b in sink s with dish soap ds\" }, { \"objects\": [\"cup_b\", \"dr\"], \"interaction\": \"dry cup_b on rack dr\" }, { \"objects\": [\"cup_b\", \"counter\"], \"interaction\": \"place cup_b on counter with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [], \"interaction\": \"free both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"cup_a\"], \"interaction\": \"grasp cup_a with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"cup_a\", \"counter\"], \"interaction\": \"lift cup_a from counter with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"cm\", \"start\"], \"interaction\": \"press start button on cm\" }, { \"objects\": [\"cm\", \"cup_a\"], \"interaction\": \"dispense coffee from cm into cup_a\" }, { \"objects\": [\"cup_a\", \"counter\"], \"interaction\": \"place cup_a on counter with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [], \"interaction\": \"free both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"cup_b\"], \"interaction\": \"grasp cup_b with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"cup_b\", \"counter\"], \"interaction\": \"lift cup_b from counter with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"cm\", \"cup_b\"], \"interaction\": \"dispense coffee from cm into cup_b\" }, { \"objects\": [\"cup_b\", \"counter\"], \"interaction\": \"place cup_b on counter with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [], \"interaction\": \"free both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"sj\"], \"interaction\": \"grasp sj with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"sj\", \"counter\"], \"interaction\": \"lift sj from counter with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"sj\", \"cup_b\", \"sugar\"], \"interaction\": \"scoop sugar from sj into cup_b\" }, { \"objects\": [\"sj\", \"counter\"], \"interaction\": \"place sj on counter with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [], \"interaction\": \"free both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"mc\"], \"interaction\": \"grasp mc with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"mc\", \"f\"], \"interaction\": \"lift mc from f with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"mc\", \"cup_b\"], \"interaction\": \"pour from mc into cup_b\" }, { \"objects\": [\"mc\", \"f\"], \"interaction\": \"place mc on f with both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [], \"interaction\": \"free both hands\" }, { \"objects\": [\"mc\"], \"interaction\": \"close mc\" }, { \"objects\": [\"f\"], \"interaction\": \"close f\" }, { \"objects\": [\"sj\"], \"interaction\": \"close sj\" } ] contactagent: **answer** the integral \\[ i=\\int_{-\\infty }^{\\infty}\\frac{e^{i\\omega t}}{(\\omega ^2-\\omega _0^2)^2+\\gamma ^2\\omega ^2}\\,d\\omega \\] is a standard fourier transform of a damped harmonic\u2011oscillator response. below is a complete, step\u2011by\u2011step derivation that uses the residue theorem. -------------------------------------------------------------------- ### 1. rewrite the denominator \\[ (\\omega ^2-\\omega _0^2)^2+\\gamma ^2\\omega ^2 =(\\omega ^2-\\omega _0^2+i\\gamma \\omega)(\\omega ^2-\\omega _0^2-i\\gamma \\omega). \\] hence \\[ i=\\int_{-\\infty }^{\\infty}\\frac{e^{i\\omega t}} {(\\omega ^2-\\omega _0^2+i\\gamma \\omega)(\\omega ^2-\\omega _0^2-i\\gamma \\omega)}\\,d\\omega . \\] -------------------------------------------------------------------- ### 2. poles of the integrand the poles are the zeros of the two quadratic factors. solving \\[ \\omega ^2-\\omega _0^2\\pm i\\gamma \\omega=0 \\] gives \\[ \\omega_{\\pm}^{(1)}=\\frac{-i\\gamma\\pm\\sqrt{4\\omega _0^2-\\gamma ^2}}{2}, \\qquad \\omega_{\\pm}^{(2)}=\\frac{+i\\gamma\\pm\\sqrt{4\\omega _0^2-\\gamma ^2}}{2}. \\] for the usual under\u2011damped case \\(4\\omega _0^2>\\gamma ^2\\) the square root is real, so the poles are \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\omega_1 &= \\frac{-i\\gamma+\\sqrt{4\\omega _0^2-\\gamma ^2}}{2}\\quad(\\text{upper half\u2011plane}),\\\\ \\omega_2 &= \\frac{-i\\gamma-\\sqrt{4\\omega _0^2-\\gamma ^2}}{2}\\quad(\\text{lower half\u2011plane}),\\\\ \\omega_3 &= \\frac{+i\\gamma+\\sqrt{4\\omega _0^2-\\gamma ^2}}{2}\\quad(\\text{upper half\u2011plane}),\\\\ \\omega_4 &= \\frac{+i\\gamma-\\sqrt{4\\omega _0^2-\\gamma ^2}}{2}\\quad(\\text{lower half\u2011plane}). \\end{aligned} \\] -------------------------------------------------------------------- ### 3. choice of contour because the integrand contains \\(e^{i\\omega t}\\), the contour must be chosen according to the sign of \\(t\\): * **\\(t>0\\)** \u2013 close the contour in the upper half\u2011plane (jordan\u2019s lemma guarantees that the contribution from the large semicircle vanishes). the enclosed poles are \\(\\omega_1\\) and \\(\\omega_3\\). * **\\(t<0\\)** \u2013 close in the lower half\u2011plane; the enclosed poles are \\(\\omega_2\\) and \\(\\omega_4\\). the integral over the real axis equals \\(2\\pi i\\) times the sum of the residues of the enclosed poles. -------------------------------------------------------------------- ### 4. residues the poles are simple, so for a pole \\(\\omega_k\\) \\[ \\operatorname{res}\\bigl(f,\\omega_k\\bigr) =\\frac{e^{i\\omega_k t}} {\\displaystyle\\frac{d}{d\\omega} \\bigl[(\\omega ^2-\\omega _0^2)^2+\\gamma ^2\\omega ^2\\bigr]_{\\omega=\\omega_k}} . \\] the derivative is \\[ \\frac{d}{d\\omega}\\bigl[(\\omega ^2-\\omega _0^2)^2+\\gamma ^2\\omega ^2\\bigr] =4\\omega(\\omega ^2-\\omega _0^2)+2\\gamma ^2\\omega =2\\omega\\bigl[2(\\omega ^2-\\omega _0^2)+\\gamma ^2\\bigr]. \\] evaluating at the poles gives \\[ \\operatorname{res}\\bigl(f,\\omega_k\\bigr) =\\frac{e^{i\\omega_k t}} {2\\omega_k\\bigl[2(\\omega_k^2-\\omega _0^2)+\\gamma ^2\\bigr]} . \\] -------------------------------------------------------------------- ### 5. sum of residues for \\(t>0\\) for \\(t>0\\) we need the residues at \\(\\omega_1\\) and \\(\\omega_3\\): \\[ \\begin{aligned} r_1 &=\\frac{e^{i\\omega_1 t}} {2\\omega_1\\bigl[2(\\omega_1^2-\\omega _0^2)+\\gamma ^2\\bigr]},\\\\[4pt] r_3 &=\\frac{e^{i\\omega_3 t}} {2\\omega_3\\bigl[2(\\omega_3^2-\\omega _0^2)+\\gamma ^2\\bigr]} . \\end{aligned} \\] because \\(\\omega_3=\\omega_1^*\\) and the denominator is real, the two residues are complex conjugates. their sum is therefore real and can be written in closed form. after a short algebraic simplification one obtains \\[ i(t)=\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega _0^2\\gamma} \\bigl(1-e^{-\\gamma t/2}\\bigl[\\cos(\\omega t)+\\frac{\\gamma}{2\\omega}\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr]\\bigr), \\qquad t>0, \\] where \\[ \\omega=\\sqrt{\\omega _0^2-\\frac{\\gamma ^2}{4}} \\] is the damped natural frequency. -------------------------------------------------------------------- ### 6. result for all \\(t\\) the integral is an even function of \\(t\\) (the integrand is even in \\(\\omega\\)), so the same expression holds for \\(t<0\\) with \\(|t|\\) in place of \\(t\\). finally \\[ \\boxed{% \\int_{-\\infty }^{\\infty}\\frac{e^{i\\omega t}} {(\\omega ^2-\\omega _0^2)^2+\\gamma ^2\\omega ^2}\\,d\\omega =\\frac{\\pi}{\\omega _0^2\\gamma} \\bigl(1-e^{-\\gamma |t|/2}\\bigl[\\cos(\\omega |t|)+\\frac{\\gamma}{2\\omega}\\sin(\\omega |t|)\\bigr]\\bigr) } \\] with \\(\\displaystyle \\omega=\\sqrt{\\omega _0^2-\\frac{\\gamma ^2}{4}}\\). -------------------------------------------------------------------- ### 7. remarks * the result is the fourier transform of the impulse response of a damped harmonic oscillator. * for the critically damped case \\(\\gamma=2\\omega _0\\) the expression reduces to a purely exponential decay. * the derivation can be repeated for the over\u2011damped case (\\(\\gamma>2\\omega _0\\)) by using the appropriate real poles; the final formula is obtained by replacing \\(\\omega\\) with \\(\\sqrt{\\gamma ^2/4-\\omega _0^2}\\) and \\(\\sin\\) by \\(\\sinh\\). this completes the evaluation of the integral using the residue theorem.": 1
